<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.4.1: Perfect Squares with Coefficients Other than 1</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02387</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.4.1: Perfect Squares with Coefficients Other than 1</md:title>
    <md:uuid>a53f8feb-cd8f-4b62-a080-cf735e971752</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00001">Elena says, "\((x+3)^2\) can be expanded into \(x^2+6x+9\). Likewise, \((2x+3)^2\) can be expanded into \(4x^2+6x+9\)."</para>
            <para id="para-00002">Find an error in Elena's statement and correct the error. Show your reasoning.</para>
            <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00005">\((2x+3)^2\) is not equivalent to \(4x^2+6x+9\).</para>
            <para id="para-00006">Applying the <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">distributive property</term> to \((2x+3)(2x+3)\) gives \(4x^2+6x+6x+9\), which is \(4x^2+12x+9\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content>
</document>